-- The half that needs a network, and the reason the example exists.
--
--   drt run live.dlua --config allowlist.json
--
-- IN v0.4.0 THIS ABORTS. The refusals in `app.dlua` are answered before
-- anything is connected, so they work; a URL the allowlist permits reaches
-- the part of the connector that opens a socket, and `drt run` drives
-- connectors without the async runtime that part needs. The process ends
-- with a panic rather than a reply. Nothing in this file or in the config
-- works around it -- it is the runtime, not the program. The README says
-- so too, and this comment goes away when it does.
--
-- Read it anyway, next to `allowlist.json`, because between them they are
-- the whole lesson.

-- One call. Note what is absent: no header, no token, no user-agent, and
-- nothing that could obtain one. GitHub refuses a request that carries no
-- user-agent, so this call does not work without a header -- and the
-- header is in the deployment, injected on the way out by the connector
-- and not echoed back in the reply. The program calls the API on terms it
-- can neither read nor change.
--
-- Swap that injected `user-agent` for an `authorization` and nothing here
-- changes. That is a capability model doing its actual job: not gating a
-- call by name, but holding the credential on the other side of the
-- boundary from the code that spends it.
local resp, status, detail = host.try("rest/get", { url = "https://api.github.com/zen" })

if status ~= "ok" then
    print("rest/get  " .. status .. "  " .. tostring(detail))
    return
end

-- `status` above is the reply's status -- whether the call was permitted
-- and completed. `resp.status` is HTTP's, and a 404 or a 500 is a
-- perfectly successful call that got an unhappy answer. Two different
-- questions, deliberately not merged.
print("http           " .. resp.status)
print("content-type   " .. tostring(resp.content_type))
print("body           " .. tostring(resp.body))
